--TEST--
`Core\Test\Failure` descends from `Throwable` directly, so neither a `LogicError` nor a `RuntimeError` clause admits it and only the root does, and a `Core\Test\Failure` clause admits nothing but its own — `rule:testing/failure-ledger`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Test\Failure
// The place in spec § 10's tree, with no runner and no ledger anywhere near it:
// what this pins is which `catch` clause a failed check reaches, which
// `crates/nvs-hir/src/errors.rs` decides on its own. A test's failures are not
// the program's errors, and this is the assertion that says so.
use Core\Test\Failure;

final class Placement {
    // Neither branch of the tree a running program throws into: a check that
    // did not hold is not a mistake in the code and not the world saying no, so
    // the clauses written for those two must not take it.
    public static function clause(): string {
        try {
            throw new Failure("the basket should have been emptied");
        } catch (LogicError $notAMistake) {
            return "a LogicError clause";
        } catch (RuntimeError $notTheWorldSayingNo) {
            return "a RuntimeError clause";
        } catch (Throwable $theRoot) {
            return "only a Throwable clause";
        }
    }

    // And by its own name, which is what a helper written around a failed check
    // uses — the rule makes catching one necessary rather than merely allowed.
    public static function byName(): string {
        try {
            throw new Failure("the record had three wrong fields");
        } catch (Failure $failed) {
            return $failed->message;
        }
    }

    // The bound from the other side: a clause written for a failed check does
    // not quietly take an error the program itself raised, so a helper that
    // gathers failures never swallows a bug in the code under test.
    public static function notAProgramError(): string {
        try {
            throw new LogicError("the call was wrong");
        } catch (Failure $wrong) {
            return "a Failure clause";
        } catch (Throwable $right) {
            return "not a Failure clause";
        }
    }

    // A frame between the throw and the caller that re-raises the same value
    // hands on the same message, which is what lets a wrapper report a failure
    // it decided not to handle.
    public static function rethrown(): string {
        try {
            try {
                throw new Failure("the two amounts did not match");
            } catch (Failure $passingThrough) {
                throw $passingThrough;
            }
        } catch (Failure $outside) {
            return $outside->message;
        }
    }
}

echo Placement::clause(), "\n";
echo Placement::byName(), "\n";
echo Placement::notAProgramError(), "\n";
echo Placement::rethrown(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
only a Throwable clause
the record had three wrong fields
not a Failure clause
the two amounts did not match
